WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.540
TARUN JAIN: Good
morning, everyone,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.956
and thank you for being here
for an early morning session.

00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:14.410
I know it's been a long
week for a lot of you.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:18.430
I hope the Google Next was
fun and exciting for you.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.050
For me, personally,
it was pretty

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.050
inspiring to see how far
the ecosystem has evolved,

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.730
and all the
opportunities it kind of

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.340
presents to our partners,
to our developers,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.950
and to our end users.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.310
The topic today we're going to
talk about is Google Assistant,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.920
and how to build your
experiences on Google

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:36.820
Assistant.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.250
I'm Tarun Jain, and
I'm a product manager

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.430
on Google Assistant.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.680
MATTHEW CARROLL: And I'm Matt.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.140
I'm a developer
programs engineer

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.260
on the Google Assistant.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.570
TARUN JAIN: So
let's jump right in.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.900
So when we think of
Google Assistant,

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.429
there are few trends that
I'm going to talk about

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:55.220
before we set the stage
on how do you build

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.950
your app for Google Assistant.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.160
We are at an amazing point in
technology where we are really

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.040
pushing the envelope
on what can technology

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.290
do to make things
happen for the users,

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.770
to help them do things in a
lot more seamless and efficient

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:10.880
way.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.710
The first thing is
that interactions are

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.400
more natural than ever before.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.620
So what does that mean?

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.020
If I think of a simple task,
like I'm taking a very, very

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.310
simple example as, do I need to
take an umbrella to work today?

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.630
Or I drive up from
the peninsula,

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.800
so do I need to carry my
jacket to San Francisco?

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.410
And so on.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.940
How do you think about it?

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.070
You check what the weather
is, what time of the day

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.840
it's probably going to rain.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.280
This probably
aligns with the time

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.610
I actually take a walk down
for my commute to the train.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.380
I should carry an
umbrella, right?

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.680
Now imagine Google Assistant
doing that for you, which is,

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.680
it has access to what your
calendar is, what your schedule

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.090
looks like for the day.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.489
It knows how often and
what times of the day

00:01:49.489 --> 00:01:50.780
do you actually take the train.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.600
It knows what the weather
is for the rest of the day,

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.170
and can help you and
can recommend to you

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:57.999
that you need to carry
an umbrella today.

00:01:57.999 --> 00:02:00.290
And then the next thing is
kind of being more proactive

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:01.780
about it.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.090
A very, very simple task, but
we have the building blocks

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.160
to do some of these things.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.430
Now imagine, what should
I order for food tonight?

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.759
Or what my weekend
plans should be,

00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:15.050
or which movie I should be
watching this Friday, and so

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:15.549
on.

00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:20.180
So the user expectations
around interaction with Google

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.980
and on other
surfaces that we have

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.070
are becoming a lot more natural.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.140
The second thing I
want to talk about

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.270
is the Assistant platform.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:33.780
The Assistant platforms are
becoming a lot more mainstream.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.330
So think back a few years ago.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.780
If you want to do something,
you want to go somewhere,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.020
you want to buy something, you
would go to your mobile phone

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.850
or to your desktop
or your laptop

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.040
based on where you
are to look that up

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.260
and kind of do that task.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.810
But now with Google
Assistant, you

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.910
have Google Home
sitting in your kitchen.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.400
You have Android TV, that
is Assistant powered,

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.540
in your living room.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.580
You have Android Auto when
you're driving, and so on.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.050
So users are using all
these computing touchpoints

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.260
to actually get things done
no matter where they are.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.204
They want to be able to
talk to their brands,

00:03:08.204 --> 00:03:09.620
they want to be
able to get things

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.500
done while they're actually
busy cooking in the kitchen.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.530
So imagine your hands are
occupied while you're cooking.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.320
You want to be able to ask
Google Home at that point

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.800
of time is that, what do I do
next with the pasta I'm making?

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.000
And so on.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.030
So Assistant is becoming
lot more mainstream.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.220
These computing
touchpoints are everywhere,

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:31.790
and they're growing as we speak.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.360
And both of these are feeding
into how the user experience

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.430
and expectations are changing.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:38.570
The needs are evolving.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:43.670
We see users becoming a lot more
curious, a lot more demanding,

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:45.800
and a lot more
impatient with what

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.680
they expect from the platform.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.790
Obviously personalized.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:53.210
Lot more users expect their
experiences to be personalized.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:57.120
You might think this is obvious
and you've seen this before.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.300
But from it being
a nice-to-have,

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.240
personalization is
becoming our table stakes.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.000
So I'll give an example.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.730
Just the Google queries that
we see which end with "for me,"

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.670
like "the best restaurant for
me" or "weekend plans for me"

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.800
have grown up 60%
in the last year.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.910
And that's a big number
based on the denominator

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.870
we are talking about.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.959
Users expect their experience
to be a lot more real time.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:23.990
They want to be able to get
things done there and then.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.950
A few anecdotes here.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.950
When you think of shopping,
more and more people

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:28.970
want same day delivery.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.220
More and more people want,
actually, two hour delivery

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.180
windows.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.330
When it comes to travel,
the same day travel category

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.050
has grown up by more than
100% in the last year or so,

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.070
which means people are looking
a lot more for hotels to book

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.830
tonight or flights they want to
today or tomorrow, and so on.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.630
So actions and tasks that
people want to accomplish

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.280
are becoming a lot
more real time,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.710
are becoming a lot more, I
want it in the next 24 hours,

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.600
and so on.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.790
And with that comes
everywhere they are.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.490
Users want things to be done
no matter where they are.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.310
And as I talked about, as
these computing touchpoints are

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.689
becoming omnipresent, they
could be in your home,

00:05:08.689 --> 00:05:10.980
in your living room, in your
car, in a washing machine,

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.480
everywhere.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.220
They want to be able to
interact with the right partners

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:17.070
at that point of time
to get things done.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.320
They are seeking information
no matter where they are.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.130
And we have touchpoints with
users throughout their day.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.690
So no matter where.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.840
So if you think of
your full day schedule,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.000
in the morning during
the morning routine,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.610
people reach out to Google Home
to check what their day looks

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:41.410
like, what the calendar is,
top news, talk updates, music,

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:42.000
and so on.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.340
During the commute,
they use Google Maps

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.930
to go where they are going.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.510
During the day, it's
about Google email,

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.940
it's about their calendar,
it's about Google search

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.200
for productivity.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.050
And in the evening as
they are winding down,

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.890
it's about playing
something on YouTube,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:03.600
or even playing a few apps and
games on the Android ecosystem.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.330
So we see users use their
experiences with Google

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.050
services throughout the day.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.190
And users expect all those
experiences to be connected.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.850
So if you think
through the whole day,

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.610
they move from Google
Home device to,

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.240
let's say, a mobile phone
to their car to a laptop

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.910
and back to the living
room TV and so on.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.880
And the expectation is that
all these experiences are

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:34.710
connected, they seamlessly flow
from one surface to another,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:41.130
and these experiences
are seamless.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.770
And one of the big goals
that we have with Assistant

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:46.200
is to help users
get things done.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.470
How do we actually provide
enough value to users

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.510
where we are doing
things for them?

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.730
So we have three
customer journeys

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.100
that we prioritize
and we focus on.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.560
So when we actively
think of use cases that

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.250
matter for our users, they fall
in one of these three buckets.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.709
So streamline your
morning at home.

00:07:03.709 --> 00:07:05.208
How do we make sure
that when you're

00:07:05.208 --> 00:07:07.650
trying to get out of your
house, we can actually help you

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.260
by getting things done so
you have less to worry about

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.679
and you can move out
of the house quickly?

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:14.220
So these could be
very simple things,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.670
like turn off the thermostat
when I'm leaving, to something

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.270
a little bit more
fancy, which is

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:23.220
order my coffee so that I can
pick it up on the way to work.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.030
So that's one big
area of focus for us,

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:29.250
and we'll go into this in
a little bit more detail.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.020
Then do more while
you are on the go.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.540
You could be on a commute,
you could be driving.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.070
How do we help
you do more things

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:38.130
while you are in that mode?

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.670
And then finally, have a
relaxing evening at home.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:43.090
So when you are unwinding,
when you are back home,

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:45.400
you want to spend time
with family and so on.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:48.170
How do we take off some of the
mundane chores off your hands

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:52.260
and do it for you so that you
get to spend time doing things

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.680
that you really want to do?

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.560
So here's an example.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.970
We launched Starbucks on
Assistant a few weeks ago.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:06.320
And this is a very good use
case that Starbucks implemented.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.420
"Order my usual from Starbucks."

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:09.470
"OK, getting Starbucks."

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.094
It asks you for the drink
that you want, the location,

00:08:12.094 --> 00:08:13.760
and places the order
for you so that you

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.630
have a coffee ready for you by
the time you get to the shop.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.850
So Starbucks realized
that they have

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.810
a lot of users who
come to their stores

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:25.430
and order the same
coffee everyday.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.390
It's pretty much like a
morning habit for a lot of us.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:31.280
And on an Assistant
Service, they

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.340
picked up a use case
that matches or aligns

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.179
with that morning habit.

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.169
It is less about, you can come
in and order anything you want,

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:41.299
and all the other fancy
scenarios that you

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:43.100
would want to be there.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.080
But they focused
on a specific use

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.170
case, which is, this
is what a morning

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:51.830
routine for a lot of users look
like, is I just want my order.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.910
This is the usual
coffee I have every day.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.939
And knowing all the
customizations they offer,

00:08:56.939 --> 00:08:59.230
this is what the user orders
every day in the mornings.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.820
So can we make it
seamless for the user?

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.130
Can we add that convenience
and value to the user?

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:09.520
And this is the examples
that I'm talking about.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.630
The ecosystem is
evolving very fast.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.710
We have thousands and thousands
of apps on Assistant now

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:18.250
which are actively building a
lot of brands on the platform.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:21.580
We believe this is the right
time for our developers

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.280
to start building their
experiences on Assistant.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.160
So why is this the right time?

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.570
Assistant is on 500
million plus devices,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:34.370
on 5,000 plus different kinds
of devices that are out there.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.960
So it includes the
voice only devices.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:40.720
It includes the phones which
have a screen and voice.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:42.390
And then through the
rest of the year,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.140
we are launching things like
smart screens and so on.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:50.410
So there is a big distribution
of the devices that we have.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.950
It's a big value add
for developers to be

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.340
an early mover in the vertical.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:55.930
They are seen as an innovator.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.330
And a good way for them
to set the precedent

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.750
on what a great experience
in their vertical looks like.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.240
It's a great way for
developers and for brands

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:07.580
to connect with new users
who are on that platform.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.660
But also add a lot of delight
for their loyal customers,

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.300
or for their repeat customers.

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:16.010
And then, as I said, this
is a two-year-old platform.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.170
So this is evolving.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.360
We are making a lot
of progress on this.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.280
So you get to be part of a fast
evolving platform, and shape

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:27.190
what that looks like based
on the businesses you are in.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.560
So I think with this,
I'll hand it over to Matt.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.810
He'll walk us through,
how do you actually

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:35.210
go and build an experience
like that on Assistant?

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:36.640
MATTHEW CARROLL: Thanks, Tarun.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:38.200
So the next 20
minutes or so, I'm

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:39.850
going to go over all
the steps you need

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:41.480
to do to build your own action.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.390
And everything I'm
going to go over today

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.430
you can start building today.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.940
So first, what are we
going to walk through here?

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:53.260
On the left, you can see a
screen of an Assistant action

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:54.140
directory page.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.050
So this is what
your users will see

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:57.880
when you share out your action.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.380
And we're going to
go over an action

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.050
called Which Language,
which tells people

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:03.760
about programming languages.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.440
The other two
screenshots you can see

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.700
are users actually
interacting with your action,

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.070
getting information, and
scrolling through lists,

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:15.080
and talking to the action.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.540
So actions are conversational.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.190
So what's the
actual conversation

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:20.510
that we want our users to have?

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:23.765
So first, the user will talk to
the Google Assistant and say,

00:11:23.765 --> 00:11:25.390
I would like to talk
to Which Language.

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:27.730
Or you can replace
the name of your app

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:30.360
once you've built it, talk
to the name of your action.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.860
And then the Google Assistant
will hand off the conversation

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:33.730
to our action.

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.100
The action will have a
chance to introduce itself,

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.700
tell the user what it can do.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.670
And then the conversation
will continue

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.180
with the user responding
and your action responding

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.170
until the user has the
information they need

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:47.420
or the action accomplished.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.050
So how does this
work on the back end?

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.130
So once a user talks
to the Google Assistant

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:53.180
and indicates they want
to talk to an action,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.870
the Google Assistant will route
that request to your action.

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.600
You'll have a chance to do
any API calls, database look

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.282
ups, whatever you need to do to
accomplish whatever the user is

00:12:02.282 --> 00:12:02.990
asking you to do.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.450
And then respond with the
information or the response

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:06.800
you'd like to send to the user.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:09.940
And then that will be forwarded
through the Google Assistant

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.810
to the user.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:17.500
So the first step in building
an action like this is design.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:23.400
So in this kind of new space
with conversational actions,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.990
if you start with
your text stack

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.080
or start developing first,
you can end up in a position

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.809
where most of the code
you've written isn't useful.

00:12:30.809 --> 00:12:32.850
Or you can kind of develop
yourself into a corner

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.510
where a lot of the code
you have isn't useful.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.450
So design is incredibly
important with conversations.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:42.750
So make sure to
start with design.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.690
The first part of
design is a persona.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:49.990
So this is kind of the
philosophy behind your brand.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:54.010
And if you already are working
with a UX or other team that's

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.350
for your brand that has
come up with a persona,

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:59.745
this is a great
time to use that.

00:12:59.745 --> 00:13:01.620
If you don't already
have a persona

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:03.900
for your brand or your
action, the first thing

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.650
you want to start with
is just adjectives.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:07.980
So this is just
things that you want

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.390
people to think of when
they think of your action.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:14.300
So things like geeky, and
over eager, or impatient.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:17.250
They don't all have to be
very positive adjectives,

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:21.572
but something that embodies what
you're trying to get across.

00:13:21.572 --> 00:13:23.280
And from there, you
can kind of move this

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:24.790
into a character model.

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:27.240
So something that
kind of embodies

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:28.260
a lot of the adjectives.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.218
And then from there,
combine all those together

00:13:30.218 --> 00:13:34.080
to come up with a complete
description of the personality

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.210
that you'd like to convey.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.220
So after you've
decided on a persona,

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:43.050
the next step is
choosing a voice.

00:13:43.050 --> 00:13:45.930
So you can send texts
to the Google Assistant,

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.920
and that will be
translated into speech.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.930
And you have several
voices to choose from

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:54.290
to make sure it's consistent
with your persona.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.750
But for that extra
level of personality,

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.240
you can record your own
voices and audio clips

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:01.584
and send those to the
Google Assistant, which will

00:14:01.584 --> 00:14:02.750
be forwarded on to the user.

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:07.010
Or have a voice actor
record those for you.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.870
So now that we've got a persona
and we know what kind of voice

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.620
we want to have, the
next step is actually

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:17.060
writing the dialogue and
writing out the conversation

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.380
that you want to
have with the user.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:22.340
So you want to focus on
some of those use cases

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.850
that Tarun was talking about
earlier, those quick and easy

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:27.950
use cases to get something done.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:31.280
And start with very
limited use cases

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.820
and very quick interactions,
and then expand from there.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.620
And then once you
have a few sets

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:39.770
of dialogues and happy
paths for different things

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.560
that your action can do,
start creating a high level

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.092
flow of how your conversation
is going to branch,

00:14:45.092 --> 00:14:46.550
and how each of
those happy path is

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:50.150
going to play with each other.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.300
This is a great time
to revisit your persona

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:56.480
and really make sure that
the dialogue you're creating

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.827
embodies that persona, and
the personality of your action

00:14:59.827 --> 00:15:00.410
comes through.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.480
So now that we have some
dialogue, how do we build it?

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:09.900
So first, we need to set up
our development environment.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.300
And the place where
you're going to start,

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:13.860
the action is on Google Console.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.170
So this is where everything
about your action

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:18.900
lives, from the
Assistant Directory page

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:21.030
that we saw earlier,
from setting up

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.200
testing and deployment.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:25.290
You can see how your action
is doing with Analytics,

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:26.997
and setting up other
advanced features

00:15:26.997 --> 00:15:28.080
that I'm going to go over.

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:34.981
So the actions on Google
Console uses the same projects

00:15:34.981 --> 00:15:37.480
that you're used to from Google
Cloud Platform and Firebase,

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.880
so you can have all your
resources in one project

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:44.050
and easily accessible
to your action.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.270
So now that we know
where we want to start,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.300
how do we actually
understand the user?

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:51.250
So the Google Assistant
will transcribe

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.680
the voice of the user,
and sends you the text.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.140
But how do we get from
that text to understanding

00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:00.202
what the user wants, and what
the user's trying to get at?

00:16:00.202 --> 00:16:01.910
So this is where the
Dialogflow comes in.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.570
Dialogflow is a natural
language understanding platform

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.910
that allows you to understand
the intent behind what

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:10.750
the user is saying, and also
extracts important elements

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:11.800
of the user's speech out.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.360
So things like
times and dates when

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.130
you're trying to
schedule an appointment,

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:19.120
or other custom parameters
like product names.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.530
Dialogflow also helps you
manage how the dialogue actually

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:26.620
flows, and allows you to
enable the branching that we

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:30.574
talked about earlier when
you're creating your dialogue.

00:16:30.574 --> 00:16:33.060
So where does Dialogflow
fit in the stack here?

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:36.386
So after the user
says, in the case

00:16:36.386 --> 00:16:37.760
they want to talk
to your action,

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.950
that request will be
forwarded to Dialogflow.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.740
And that will trigger
the welcome intent,

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:44.720
and then we'll have
a chance to respond

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:46.969
to introduce our action.

00:16:46.969 --> 00:16:48.010
So how do we set that up?

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.570
So we start in the
Actions Console

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:51.100
here, and indicate
what languages

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:52.360
we want to start with.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.250
And then start building
with Dialogflow.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:56.470
So you start building.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.840
You create a Dialogflow
agent, and there's already

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.962
a welcome intent
created for you.

00:17:00.962 --> 00:17:02.670
You can see in the
screenshot here, we're

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.211
removing some of
the generic welcomes

00:17:04.211 --> 00:17:05.950
that just say hi
or hello, and we're

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:08.589
going to add a custom
response specifically

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:11.140
for our action that
introduces our action,

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:13.750
and kind of tells
people what they can do.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:22.849
So now you can see that we can
just write in a text response

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:23.703
into Dialogflow.

00:17:23.703 --> 00:17:25.119
So this is just a
static response.

00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:26.785
Since this isn't
really going to change,

00:17:26.785 --> 00:17:29.340
we're just going to create a
couple different variations.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:33.060
And one of these two variations
will be surfaced to the user.

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:35.370
And we'll ask them
a question of,

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:37.890
what programming language
do you want to know about,

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.160
so that they know what
our action is capable of

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:43.091
and can respond appropriately.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.320
So after we ask the
question, what language you

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:51.010
want to know about, you can
think of all the various things

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:52.282
people might say.

00:17:52.282 --> 00:17:53.740
There's probably
thousands that you

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.090
could think of if
you had enough time,

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.290
and there's probably
thousands more

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:58.456
that you wouldn't think of
that people will actually

00:17:58.456 --> 00:17:59.690
ask your action.

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:02.930
So this is really where the
power of Dialogflow comes in.

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:07.120
If we can enter all these
examples that are on the screen

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:10.930
here as training
phrases for Dialogflow,

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.280
and we can add a new intent
called the Which Language

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:16.584
intent, Dialogflow
will use these examples

00:18:16.584 --> 00:18:18.250
as training for a
machine learning model

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:20.260
so that any of these
examples will be captured

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.704
in the Which Language intent.

00:18:22.704 --> 00:18:24.370
But the machine
learning model will also

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:26.980
capture any other phrases
that the user says

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.832
that have the similar
intent of wanting to know

00:18:29.832 --> 00:18:31.040
about a programming language.

00:18:33.564 --> 00:18:35.730
So this is how we actually
add the training phrases.

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:39.080
So in this Which Language
intent that we create,

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:40.750
we'll add some
training phrases here.

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:46.010
And then Dialogflow will train
using these phrases as samples

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:48.280
so we can capture these
phrases and others.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:51.850
You'll also notice that one
of these words is highlighted.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:54.862
That is Dialogflow
recognizing an entity.

00:18:54.862 --> 00:18:57.070
So we need to know which
programming language they're

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.040
actually talking about.

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:00.415
So we'll need to extract out
the name of that programming

00:19:00.415 --> 00:19:01.010
language.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:03.420
So that's what
we'll do next here.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.930
So you can create
your own entities

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:09.320
in Dialogflow
using this UI here.

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:11.220
So we have five
programming languages

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.000
that we want to support.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:16.270
And we have them listed
on the left there.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:18.090
But we also, in
the right column,

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.800
have all the synonyms of
those programming languages.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:22.500
So if someone wanted
to say JavaScript,

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:24.916
they might be on their phone,
and they might just type JS.

00:19:24.916 --> 00:19:26.650
So we know that
probably means they're

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.610
talking about JavaScript,
so Dialogflow will interpret

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:32.550
that JS as JavaScript
and forward that along

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:35.310
to your back end
so we can do that.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.250
You can also use
CSV uploads if you

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:39.320
have a large list,
or a product catalog,

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:41.720
or a large number of
entities you want to support.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:47.570
All right, so now here's
what our stack looks like.

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:50.807
We first have the user
asking to talk to our action.

00:19:50.807 --> 00:19:52.140
The welcome intent is triggered.

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:53.720
We respond properly.

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:57.860
And then the user asks about a
specific programming language.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:59.930
That triggers our
Which Language intent.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.919
And then the information about
which language they picked

00:20:02.919 --> 00:20:04.460
is going to get sent
to our back end.

00:20:04.460 --> 00:20:07.000
And then we're
going to send them

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:11.150
the information about
that programming language.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:13.840
So how do we actually send that
information to our back end?

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.720
So Dialogflow has this
concept called fulfillment,

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:18.950
and you can set up a
web hook for fulfillment

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:23.530
where the HTTP call will
be made to any URL you

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.815
specify with information
about the request.

00:20:25.815 --> 00:20:28.660
So which intent was matched, the
original text the user actually

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:33.700
said, any parameters or entities
like the programming language

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:36.370
we defined earlier so that you
can know what the user wants

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:37.990
to do, and have
all the information

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:39.899
you need to respond to properly.

00:20:39.899 --> 00:20:41.440
To get up and running
really quickly,

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:44.200
we also have an inline editor
with Firebase functions

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:47.950
so you don't have to
set up a web server.

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:50.980
So now we're getting
into actually writing

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:53.660
some code for our fulfillments.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.210
So first, we just start with
a very simple map of the five

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:58.130
programming
languages we support,

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:00.110
and the information
that we want to send

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.270
to the user about each
one of those languages

00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:05.880
that we'll use in just a second.

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:09.920
So this is kind of a
basic template of what

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.810
you need to do in fulfillment.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:15.680
So there is an action on
Google Client Library that

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:19.130
helps us parse out the requests
coming from Dialogflow,

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.560
and also compile a
response properly

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.547
to be forwarded to the user.

00:21:23.547 --> 00:21:25.130
So first, we're
importing the library.

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:26.630
And I'm setting up
some boilerplate.

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:29.090
And then that highlighted
section is actually

00:21:29.090 --> 00:21:31.390
where we define our responses.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.550
So first, we say app.intent.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.415
So this just
defines which intent

00:21:35.415 --> 00:21:37.790
we want to respond to, if you
have multiple intents you'd

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:38.450
like to respond to.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:40.491
We only have one, and it's
called Which Language.

00:21:40.491 --> 00:21:42.089
So we just enter
in Which Language.

00:21:42.089 --> 00:21:43.380
And then we have two arguments.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:44.671
One is the conversation object.

00:21:44.671 --> 00:21:47.150
So this is what we use
to add responses that

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:48.524
will be sent back to the user.

00:21:48.524 --> 00:21:50.190
And then we have the
language parameter.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.060
So this is the parameter that
Dialogflow captured earlier

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:54.860
that has the value
JavaScript, Kotlin,

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:57.590
or any other programming
language we support.

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.510
So the first thing
we do is we take

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:03.650
that map I showed you
earlier, and take the language

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:04.850
and insert that.

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:07.520
And grab the description for
whatever programming language

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.080
the user mentioned.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:12.680
So we're adding that response
about the language first.

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.330
And then next,
we're asking if they

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:16.820
want to know about another
language in our second message

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:17.330
there.

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:19.070
And then that last
line there is just

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:22.010
to connect this to Firebase
functions, which is what

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:23.360
this example is running on.

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:30.090
So now we've got something
working end to end.

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:31.110
How do we try it out?

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:33.210
So we go back to
the Actions Console,

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:35.820
and the Actions Console
has a simulator.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.980
So this lets you
do debug and see

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:41.950
exactly what users are going to
see when they try your action.

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:44.730
So you can see here everything
from invocation of saying,

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:45.750
talk to which language.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:49.230
And your language getting
routed from the Google Assistant

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.460
to your action.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:53.490
You can see how that works,
as well as what happens when--

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.040
you can actually enter in what
your users might say and see

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:57.250
what will happen.

00:22:57.250 --> 00:22:59.970
You can also see here we have
a difference between what

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:04.087
shows up visually and what is
actually spoken to the user,

00:23:04.087 --> 00:23:06.420
as well as a lot of debug
information and helpful things

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.120
to help you figure out
if something went wrong.

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:16.170
So far, we've only
been dealing with text.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:19.590
So if this is just on a
Google Home or a speaker,

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:21.165
that is probably appropriate.

00:23:21.165 --> 00:23:22.540
You're only speaking
to the user,

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:26.520
and the Google Assistant can
only actually speak words back.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.919
But what about a lot of
the Assistant devices

00:23:28.919 --> 00:23:29.710
that have a screen?

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:32.310
So all the phones, and our
upcoming smart displays,

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:35.570
and other devices
that have screens?

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:39.360
For that, we have a few
different rich message types.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.690
So the first one here
is called a basic card.

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:44.730
So this allows you to have
a large image, a title,

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:47.090
some descriptions, as
well as a button that

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:50.360
can go to links that
can link to websites.

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:53.660
And this is in addition to
the regular text responses

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:55.680
that we defined earlier.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:57.410
So the actions on
Google Client Library

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.810
also has some nice,
convenient methods.

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:01.250
So we can just
create a new card,

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:03.560
enter in all the
different fields

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:05.007
that I mentioned earlier.

00:24:05.007 --> 00:24:06.590
And that will be
displayed to the user

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:07.631
properly when we respond.

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:12.180
So another common
type is a list.

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:17.180
So you can use lists for any
kind of product selection,

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:20.090
or whenever you want
to surface to the user

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:23.640
a small group of items and
they need to select one.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:26.940
So for this, you have the
title, and we can have pictures.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:28.880
And then there's some
pagination here so

00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:31.790
that users can select
from multiple options.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:35.060
Similarly, the actions
on Google Client Library

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:38.020
also has support
for these list items

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:41.660
so you can create lists
programmatically, and nice

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:44.160
and easily.

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:45.510
Similar to a list is a carousel.

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:48.060
So this uses the same
kind of list selection

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:52.950
as the previous rich response,
but it just surfaces images

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:55.590
a little bit more prominently.

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:59.676
So if whatever you're trying
to get the user to select

00:24:59.676 --> 00:25:01.050
is a little bit
more visual, this

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.750
is a way to surface
that to your users.

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.850
So the last part here
is a way to detect

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:12.100
what surface your users are on.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:15.299
So if they're on a speaker, you
may want to speak to the user

00:25:15.299 --> 00:25:16.840
differently than if
they have a phone

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:19.660
and can see some of these
rich responses that we have.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:24.220
So we have the capability to
detect what surface they're on,

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:28.210
if they're on a phone or if
that surface has a screen, so we

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:29.836
can respond appropriately.

00:25:33.147 --> 00:25:35.730
The last bit that doesn't apply
to our Which Language example,

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:39.630
but can be very useful,
is account linking.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:42.720
So this allows you to
link your own account

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.210
system for your products
to the Google Assistant.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:49.260
So you can log your users in and
provide contextual information

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:51.060
based on information
you have about

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:52.920
the user in their account.

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:54.420
So this is really
useful if you want

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:57.400
to provide updates or any
information from your products

00:25:57.400 --> 00:26:01.430
in the Google Assistant.

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.620
All right, now that we've
got everything working

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:07.710
and we tested it out, how do
we get this out to our users?

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:11.010
So the first is just
testing on device.

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:15.722
So once you've enabled
testing in the simulator,

00:26:15.722 --> 00:26:17.930
any device that's logged
into the same Google account

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:20.810
that you're testing on
has access to your action.

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:23.680
So you can try it out on your
phone or Google Home device

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:25.430
that's logged into the
same Google account

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:28.130
to see what your users will
actually see when they try out

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.540
your action.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.070
After you've tested
it yourself, you

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:33.930
might want to test
it with your team.

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:35.307
So we have an
alpha release where

00:26:35.307 --> 00:26:37.890
you can share it with your team
and try it out with your team,

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:43.590
make improvements, and get
ready to deploy to your users.

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.180
After you have
something that you

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:49.410
want to deploy to users publicly
or in a small beta group,

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.120
you submit your
action for review.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:55.560
And after about a day,
that will be approved.

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:59.160
And you can publish either
to a small public beta group

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:01.510
of testers or to production.

00:27:01.510 --> 00:27:03.090
You can also have
different releases

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:05.290
in each one of these
alpha/beta production channels

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:09.000
so you can provide early release
features to your beta group

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:09.840
to see how it does.

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:14.934
So now that we've gone through
how to build an action,

00:27:14.934 --> 00:27:16.350
I'm going to hand
it back to Tarun

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:18.870
to talk about how to grow
your business with it.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.760
TARUN JAIN: Thanks, Matt.

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:22.860
So once you build
a great action,

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:25.620
it's very important for
you to actually start

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:28.250
getting users engaging
with that action.

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:30.820
So the first prerequisite is
you have a good action that you

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:33.570
have built so when you
actually bring users in,

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:37.000
they are delighted and there's
a re-engagement on that.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.620
So there are three verticals,
or three different tenants,

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:41.280
that we think of
when it comes to, how

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:44.170
do you actually start driving
your business on Assistant?

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:45.460
The first one is discovery.

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:48.970
How do you get your action
discovered on Assistant?

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:50.379
The second piece
is monetization,

00:27:50.379 --> 00:27:52.670
which is primarily transactions,
physical transactions,

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.380
digital transactions, so
you can start monetizing

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:57.860
your experience on Assistant.

00:27:57.860 --> 00:27:59.160
And the third is re-engagement.

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:00.750
For your loyal
customers, customers

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:02.666
who have come and engaged
with your experience

00:28:02.666 --> 00:28:04.980
before, how do you get them
back into the experience?

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.410
And we'll talk about a
bunch of different tools

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:11.660
that we have available so you
can kind of accomplish that.

00:28:11.660 --> 00:28:14.000
So let's talk about
discovery first.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:15.730
Imagine you have
launched the app

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.040
that Matt was talking about.

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:18.623
And how do you get
more and more users

00:28:18.623 --> 00:28:22.930
to see what that app
is and find that out?

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:26.450
There are a bunch of different
ways users can do that.

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:29.050
The first one is
Assistant Directory.

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:30.940
We have Assistant
Directory on the web.

00:28:30.940 --> 00:28:35.260
We have Assistant Directory
on iOS and Android devices.

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:38.620
Think of this as the
Play Store for Android.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:40.120
This is where users
go in and look

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:43.240
for what's new, what's
trending, and so on.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:47.530
Today, around 30% of the traffic
that a lot of our third parties

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:49.380
get is from Assistant Directory.

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:52.030
So this gets a lot
of volume from users.

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:56.539
And this is how users have been
discovering a lot of new apps.

00:28:56.539 --> 00:28:58.330
So what does Assistant
Directory look like?

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:01.660
I'll just go through
that fairly quickly.

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:04.911
One big experience for users is,
they can browse the Assistant

00:29:04.911 --> 00:29:05.410
Directory.

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:06.640
They can go through it.

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:09.490
This experience is largely
timely and personalized

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:10.180
to the users.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:11.860
It's very context specific.

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:13.480
So this browsing
experience might

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:15.790
look different based
on the time of the day,

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:19.580
or based on different
users, and so on.

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:21.340
So if you think of
the browsing piece,

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:23.740
we showed element
suggestions to the users.

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.050
In the morning, you might
see order your coffee

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:27.820
or play this playlist.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:31.657
In the evening, it might be
something different, and so on.

00:29:31.657 --> 00:29:33.990
As you scroll down, there are
many different experiences

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:35.430
that show up in browsing.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:37.350
Your app could be
in What's New, it

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:41.100
could be as part of what is
trending, what is popular,

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:44.730
or what you might like
based what other experiences

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:49.230
that users have
engaged with already.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:52.620
The other way users interact
with the explore experience I

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.900
talked about is through search.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:58.140
You can actually come and
search for different apps.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:01.200
We have a taxonomy of around
400 different categories.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:02.910
When you create
your action, you'll

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:04.770
tag it to one of
these categories

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.802
or more of these categories.

00:30:06.802 --> 00:30:08.510
So when users go and
look for, let's say,

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:10.135
in the Food and Drink
category, they'll

00:30:10.135 --> 00:30:12.541
see all the apps that are
part of that category.

00:30:12.541 --> 00:30:14.040
You could also
search for something,

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:16.900
and search triggers
some of these as well.

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:19.650
So specifically when you
think of a categorical browse,

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:21.660
within Food and Drink,
there are subcategories.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:24.320
You can order food, that's
part of Food and Drink.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:27.360
And you can see examples
of Domino's there.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:30.930
But if you are in
the recipes business,

00:30:30.930 --> 00:30:32.730
that's the example of
Food Network, which

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:35.600
is part of the Food
and Drink category,

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:39.270
but is about recipes
at that point of time.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:41.900
So as I said, explore
is a big surface area

00:30:41.900 --> 00:30:43.730
where you can actually
have your app,

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:47.560
and drive traffic to your app.

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:49.980
The second one is
an action page.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:52.360
Every app needs to
have an action page.

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.420
This is where you
would come in and talk

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:55.800
about what this action does.

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:57.660
This is how you
reflect your brand.

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:00.000
And think of it as
a pitch to the user

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:02.250
on why should they
try this, right?

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:06.750
So when you think of the
action page specifically,

00:31:06.750 --> 00:31:10.380
it has a succinct image
of your brand, and so on.

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:13.116
It talks about why
should user try the app.

00:31:13.116 --> 00:31:13.980
It has ratings.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:15.500
It has recent reviews.

00:31:15.500 --> 00:31:18.830
It has stuff like what
your recent release

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:21.180
logs were, and so on.

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:25.410
But fairly importantly, it
has this try experience,

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:29.012
which is users don't have
to install anything here.

00:31:29.012 --> 00:31:30.720
They just click on
Try and they can start

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:32.937
talking to your app experience.

00:31:32.937 --> 00:31:34.770
So the inertia for
people who try out things

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.600
is relatively low.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:39.060
And that's what we see
with a lot of experiences

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:42.830
is that, if there is a good
experience on the action page,

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:45.390
a lot of people actually
end up trying that app.

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.180
So the conversion
rate, if you will,

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:52.050
are fairly high once users
end up on an action page.

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:53.840
So how do you optimize
your action page?

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:55.256
I think no point
in guessing which

00:31:55.256 --> 00:31:57.050
is a great experience
on one of these.

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:59.870
But there are four things
that we usually talk about.

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.120
Have a crisp image
and a good image.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.310
The title is important for
what you create for your app.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:08.270
Don't create long titles which
people cannot talk to or cannot

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:09.680
talk about.

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:12.800
It's important to have
a right one there.

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:15.530
Then a good description
is important.

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:19.370
And description is not like
what your company is about

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:20.620
or what you do.

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:23.090
It's about why should a
user try this experience.

00:32:23.090 --> 00:32:26.292
So I think, try to think of
it as a pitch to the user.

00:32:26.292 --> 00:32:28.250
The last one, which is
actually very important,

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:29.930
is the invocation phrases.

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:31.480
How does a user invoke this?

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:35.660
This is how you're building a
muscle memory for your users.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:37.370
No matter what name
you create, users

00:32:37.370 --> 00:32:40.700
can always say, talk to
French Bulldog Trivia, right?

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:43.010
But what are the other
kind of invocations

00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:44.830
that users can actually use?

00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:49.441
It could be "ask French
Bulldog trivia about snoring"

00:32:49.441 --> 00:32:50.690
or something like that, right?

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:51.835
Create some fun ones.

00:32:51.835 --> 00:32:53.210
Create some
interesting ones that

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:54.720
are relevant for your business.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.720
But create multiple
invocation phrases.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:33:00.502
You don't know what drives
users to keep coming back again.

00:33:00.502 --> 00:33:01.960
When you look
through our logs, you

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:03.680
will see what the
invocation phrases

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:05.990
are more popular for
users, what are not.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:09.770
And this drives all of
traffic to your app.

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:13.570
So we talked about things
that you can do on your side

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:15.440
by creating a great
action package,

00:33:15.440 --> 00:33:19.270
by creating the great user
experience on the explore.

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:22.270
The second big piece is what
we call built-in intents.

00:33:22.270 --> 00:33:25.990
We announced built-in
intents a few months ago,

00:33:25.990 --> 00:33:28.030
and we continued to build
the platform for it.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:29.860
So what is built-in intents?

00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:31.660
There are a hundred
different ways

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:34.570
people come in and ask
for the same thing based

00:33:34.570 --> 00:33:36.220
on their dialect,
based on the slangs,

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:41.175
based on their language,
and locale, and so on.

00:33:41.175 --> 00:33:43.322
These are actually
from real logs.

00:33:43.322 --> 00:33:45.530
And basically, what the user
is trying to ask for is,

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:47.020
I want to de-stress
myself, right?

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:48.610
And then there are
many different ways

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.210
that they come in and they
ask for the same thing.

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:53.350
Now, to expect a
developer like yourself

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:55.090
to think of every
possible phrase,

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:57.280
or what user might be
saying in your vertical,

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:00.670
is fairly difficult. So
what we have is a concept

00:34:00.670 --> 00:34:02.390
we call built-in intents.

00:34:02.390 --> 00:34:05.530
Built-in intents is nothing,
but it's a standard taxonomy

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:06.670
for actions.

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:08.800
This is how, when a user
says all the things we

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:10.960
saw in the last slide,
we actually translate

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:13.840
that to a single thing
saying, meditate.

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:16.120
And you can sign up for
that built-in intent.

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:18.520
We have lots of built-in
intents already in production.

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:20.199
They are available in the tool.

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:21.820
You can sign up
for any of these.

00:34:21.820 --> 00:34:23.800
And we are launching
more and more

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.750
throughout the rest of the year.

00:34:26.750 --> 00:34:28.940
It's fairly easy to sign
up for a built-in intent

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:30.790
when you go to the
Dialogflow Console.

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.429
There's a dropdown of
all the built-in intents

00:34:32.429 --> 00:34:33.262
that we've launched.

00:34:33.262 --> 00:34:37.070
And you pick and choose one or
more that are relevant to you.

00:34:37.070 --> 00:34:41.227
It's important to be right
and relevant about this.

00:34:41.227 --> 00:34:42.935
The reason is, you
can imagine if there's

00:34:42.935 --> 00:34:45.500
a lot of traffic that
is coming to your app

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:46.850
and users are bouncing off.

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:49.190
That's a bad signal to
our ranking engines.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:52.300
So you want to be picking up
the right built-in intents that

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:54.179
are relevant for your business.

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:58.120
This has been a big driver of
traffic to a lot of our apps,

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:00.560
and I think it's
important to be selecting

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:03.630
the right built-in intents.

00:35:03.630 --> 00:35:05.790
So here's an example of
what it would look like.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:08.050
There's a built-in
intent for Play Quiz.

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:10.920
A user comes in and says,
I want to play a trivia,

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:13.580
I want to play a quiz, or I
want to play a geography quiz.

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:16.070
Whatever those
invocation phrases are,

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:18.150
they all get translated
to the same thing,

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:19.997
which is, play a quiz.

00:35:19.997 --> 00:35:22.580
And then your app could be one
of these apps that participates

00:35:22.580 --> 00:35:25.820
in that action based on how
it has done in the past,

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:28.370
and personalized
results for the user.

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:31.430
We show a carousel of a few apps
with that kind of interaction,

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:35.420
and the user can play
with any of these.

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:36.660
So think of this.

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:39.980
And these are, like I
talked about earlier,

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:41.859
like if you are in
the meditation space,

00:35:41.859 --> 00:35:43.400
you are in the coffee
ordering space,

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:47.870
you are in a health
space, a banking space,

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:50.330
we have built-in intents
for a lot of these.

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:52.460
And you can sign up
for any of those.

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:57.850
The third pillar that
we have for discovery

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:00.520
is what we call action links.

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:05.810
Action links are a link to
your action or to your app.

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.740
So they're pretty much like
your website URL, or a deep link

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:11.360
into your Android
app, and so on.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.660
So once you create
an action link,

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:18.135
you can actually use
it just like your URL

00:36:18.135 --> 00:36:19.570
on any kind of surface.

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:21.130
So you can use it
in your websites.

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:22.390
You can use it in your apps.

00:36:22.390 --> 00:36:24.680
You can use it on
social platforms.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:27.760
You can use it in your marketing
campaigns, like in your emails

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:28.820
and so on.

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:30.970
You can even use it on
billboards if you want.

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:33.700
The idea, really, is this
is how you tell users

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:35.770
that I have one
more surface which

00:36:35.770 --> 00:36:38.350
is more optimized for
some specific use cases.

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:41.020
But you can actually
engage with that surface.

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:44.600
So it's important to
think on those lines.

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:47.780
Again, as I said, it's fairly
easy to create an action link.

00:36:47.780 --> 00:36:50.420
When you go to Dialogflow,
there is a section in there

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:53.300
which would actually give you a
link for each of your actions.

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:55.700
You can create separate
ones for different intents

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:57.650
that your action supports.

00:36:57.650 --> 00:37:02.030
So if you do multiple things,
like I want to track my order,

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:03.520
there could be a separate link.

00:37:03.520 --> 00:37:05.030
Versus I want to place an order.

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:05.720
That's a separate link.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:06.860
I want to reorder something.

00:37:06.860 --> 00:37:08.568
That could be a separate
link, and so on.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:14.534
Now, I talked about
discovery quite a bit.

00:37:14.534 --> 00:37:17.200
One of the big things that a lot
of developers are interested in

00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:20.080
is, how do I monetize
my experience?

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:21.580
Once I start driving
traffic to it

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:25.090
and start putting money
behind this Assistant app,

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:27.430
how do I start monetizing it?

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:30.880
So today we support physical
goods and transaction sales

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:32.140
on the platform.

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:34.390
We are live in seven
different markets--

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:40.709
US, UK, Canada, Australia,
Germany, Japan, and France.

00:37:40.709 --> 00:37:42.500
And we are rolling out
in many more markets

00:37:42.500 --> 00:37:43.840
over the rest of the year.

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:45.700
Our plan is for this
map to look a lot more

00:37:45.700 --> 00:37:48.610
green by the end of the
year, and we're launching

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:51.030
multiple markets every month.

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:53.130
When we talk about
transactions, these

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:55.629
are transactions about
physical goods and services.

00:37:55.629 --> 00:37:58.170
But these are also things like
you want to book a table, book

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:00.920
a car test drive, and
reservations, if you think,

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:04.150
or a makeover
appointment, and so on.

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:07.001
So transaction flows
are fairly standard,

00:38:07.001 --> 00:38:08.500
if you think of the
building blocks.

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:10.840
And we support all of these
building blocks today.

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:13.520
You can customize it based
on your needs, and so on.

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:16.120
But there are three
steps for the most part.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:17.800
The first one is,
you build the order,

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:20.052
or build a cart kind of thing.

00:38:20.052 --> 00:38:21.510
This is where you
figure out what's

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:22.968
the product your
user wants to buy.

00:38:22.968 --> 00:38:24.737
You upsell, cross-sell
other things.

00:38:24.737 --> 00:38:26.320
What's the reservation
the user wants?

00:38:26.320 --> 00:38:28.278
What time works for them,
and things like that.

00:38:28.278 --> 00:38:29.690
So think on those lines.

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:31.960
But the first step is,
you build an order.

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:35.060
Once you build an order, you
propose the order to the user.

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:36.277
Are you ready to confirm?

00:38:36.277 --> 00:38:38.360
Or this is the right payment
method you are using,

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:38.860
and so on.

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:41.640
So you confirm the order,
the transaction goes through.

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:43.240
And the third piece
is very important

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:45.070
when it comes to an
assistant surface,

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:47.350
is send updates on the order.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:50.200
We have APIs where you can
push an update to orders.

00:38:50.200 --> 00:38:52.880
For the reservation, your
reservation has been confirmed.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:54.410
Or your table is now ready.

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:57.220
Or sorry we had to push out the
reservation by half an hour,

00:38:57.220 --> 00:38:58.410
and things like that.

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:00.460
If it's a transaction,
things like,

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.250
your package has been shipped,
or it's arrived, and so on are

00:39:03.250 --> 00:39:06.190
important to send to the
user more proactively.

00:39:06.190 --> 00:39:09.820
We support multiple helpers to
make it easy for our developers

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:10.760
to build transactions.

00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:13.970
There's a location helper if
you want the current location

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:14.890
of the user.

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:17.140
We have address helpers
if you want the delivery

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:18.400
address of the user.

00:39:18.400 --> 00:39:20.380
We have payment helpers
where we can actually

00:39:20.380 --> 00:39:24.640
help with payment instruments
Google has on file for users.

00:39:24.640 --> 00:39:27.760
We have payment credentials for
hundreds of millions of users

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:30.070
through our Google Play
Google Pay offering,

00:39:30.070 --> 00:39:32.920
and you can use those helpers.

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:35.760
Matt talked about you also
have account linking helpers

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:42.040
to make it easy for you to link
accounts with Google users.

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:44.070
We are also piloting
digital goods.

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:46.000
Digital goods are
also another way

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:48.740
developers can monetize
their experiences.

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:50.350
So when I talk
about digital goods,

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:52.090
think of in-app digital goods.

00:39:52.090 --> 00:39:54.280
Like if you're in
the gaming industry,

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:57.070
you can sell coins and
gems and extra lives,

00:39:57.070 --> 00:39:59.560
turns, playtime, and
things like that.

00:39:59.560 --> 00:40:04.000
And that's a fairly big business
when it comes to digital goods.

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:07.040
We also announced support
subscription services,

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:09.040
which is if you're in the
premium content space,

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:09.980
like news.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:13.680
Or premium subscriptions,
premium services.

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:16.150
This is one way to monetize
your experience, which

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:17.500
is through subscriptions.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:19.300
Or music, for that matter.

00:40:19.300 --> 00:40:22.390
And then the third
thing is digital assets,

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:24.670
like a digital book, or
movies, and stuff like that.

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:26.950
You can sell that on
the platform, too.

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:29.050
And that's other digital
goods that we support.

00:40:29.050 --> 00:40:32.350
So across physical goods,
services, reservations,

00:40:32.350 --> 00:40:34.660
digital goods, we
have a good portfolio

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:37.390
of ways our developers
can come in and create

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:40.200
transactional
experiences on Assistant.

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:41.950
When you actually
figure out the right use

00:40:41.950 --> 00:40:44.810
case that works for a user
in Assistant surfaces,

00:40:44.810 --> 00:40:47.970
this is something that helps
you monetize those experiences

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:48.595
for your users.

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:53.170
And then the last
piece I'll talk

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:55.780
about is, how do you drive
re-engagement for your users?

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:57.280
You have loyal
customers or you have

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.380
customers that have tried
your experience once.

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:00.880
How do you bring them back?

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:02.980
And what are the
platform constructs

00:41:02.980 --> 00:41:04.870
we support so you
can bring users back

00:41:04.870 --> 00:41:07.350
into your experience?

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:09.290
This is the same slide
I had earlier on,

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:11.990
is we have touchpoints with
users throughout the day.

00:41:11.990 --> 00:41:13.900
And we offer channels
to developers

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:16.630
so that they can reach back to
the user at different points

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:17.950
of this journey.

00:41:17.950 --> 00:41:20.690
So if you are a
use case where you

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:23.230
want more and more users
engage in the morning,

00:41:23.230 --> 00:41:25.210
we have different ways
you can actually become

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:26.470
part of that habit for users.

00:41:26.470 --> 00:41:28.720
Or throughout the day, or
through the evening routine,

00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:29.690
and so on.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:33.680
So let me talk about
an example of that.

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:35.710
We have something
that we call routines.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:37.120
We have a lot of
default routines

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:38.960
that we have created for users.

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:42.040
Routines is nothing, but
it is multiple actions

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:44.105
bundled into a single command.

00:41:44.105 --> 00:41:45.730
So from a user's
perspective, let's say

00:41:45.730 --> 00:41:49.980
user says good morning, or,
hey, Google, good morning.

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:53.000
It kicks off a series
of actions for the user.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:54.370
So it can turn off their heat.

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:57.700
It can move their phone
out of the silent mode.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:00.970
It tells the user about their
top news, their stock portfolio

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:02.370
updates.

00:42:02.370 --> 00:42:04.960
It tells about their
calendar, how their commute is

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:07.240
going to be to work, and so on.

00:42:07.240 --> 00:42:09.700
And a user can configure
it, set it up, and so on.

00:42:09.700 --> 00:42:12.040
But that's just one
example of a routine.

00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:16.450
And you as a developer has a way
to become part of the routines,

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:19.060
like a tip of the day, or
the thought of the day.

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:22.870
Or how long is the
wait line at Caltrain?

00:42:22.870 --> 00:42:24.170
And things like that.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:26.680
So this is one way where
you can proactively

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:29.920
be part of users' journeys,
or your action can

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:32.780
be a proactive part
of users' journeys.

00:42:32.780 --> 00:42:36.120
And there are many more
that we have out there.

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:38.150
This is a big engagement
point for users today

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:41.660
where we see users using
these routines a lot.

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:44.070
Because it helps them
make certain parts

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:47.780
of their day a lot more
seamless and efficient.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:49.880
A fairly easy way for
you, the developer,

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:52.640
to sign up for
intents or for any

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:56.510
of these routines, if you go
to the console, it's an opt-in.

00:42:56.510 --> 00:42:58.550
You sign up saying,
I want my action

00:42:58.550 --> 00:43:01.080
to be eligible for
any of these routines.

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:03.260
And you turn on
that radio button,

00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:05.720
and your option
shows up to users

00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:08.750
to be part of that routine.

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:12.720
There are a couple of other
ways that we support or drive

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:13.370
re-engagement.

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:16.330
The first one is daily updates.

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:18.970
The thing with daily updates
is that these are the updates

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:22.000
that Google sends
to the user saying,

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.220
we think this is a relevant
experience for you.

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:27.280
Or this is a relevant
call to action for you.

00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:29.980
We work with developers
to figure out

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:32.110
what are the right ones
to send to the user.

00:43:32.110 --> 00:43:35.540
And that's one way where
we can be proactive.

00:43:35.540 --> 00:43:37.640
The second piece is
push notifications.

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:39.460
This is like the
push notifications

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:41.740
you have on the app ecosystem.

00:43:41.740 --> 00:43:44.200
Users opt into getting push
notifications from you,

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:46.090
like the example
from "Esquire" here.

00:43:46.090 --> 00:43:48.340
And users will
proactively see every time

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:50.300
you want to push an update.

00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:52.420
So this could be an
update in terms of,

00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:54.740
there is a new
product available.

00:43:54.740 --> 00:43:57.970
Or you signed up for a
certain deal that's available,

00:43:57.970 --> 00:43:59.800
or a deal of the day,
or whatever it is.

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:02.260
So you can think of what is
relevant for your business,

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:04.360
but you can start
pushing out notifications

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:07.690
for the user through
various Google surfaces,

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:10.290
not just on the Assistant side.

00:44:10.290 --> 00:44:12.150
So that's all that
I had in terms of,

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:14.980
how do you drive
re-engagement for your users?

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:16.590
We have a lot of
assets and resources

00:44:16.590 --> 00:44:19.470
out there that you can use
to learn more about this.

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:21.200
We have best
practices on design,

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:22.920
how to create the right design.

00:44:22.920 --> 00:44:25.020
Example is that you
want to sell tickets.

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:26.820
We have examples
on how you do that.

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:28.920
You want to create
games, we have

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:30.840
examples on how to create that.

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:32.970
And for different parts of
the different verticals,

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:34.920
we have a lot of
example code, example

00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:36.750
designs that you can use.

00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:38.800
And then, you can sign
up for a mailing list

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:42.087
to stay updated with all the
new and upcoming launches.

00:44:42.087 --> 00:44:43.170
I think that's all we had.

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:45.890
And thank you so
much for your time.

